<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kwaku Dua I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1797–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Kwaku Dua Panin, Fredua Agyeman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kwaku Dua I, also Kwaku Dua Panin and born Fredua Agyeman, was the eighth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante Empire</a>, from 1834 until his death in 1867. He had fought against Gyaman and commanded a division at the battle of Katamanso in 1826. As king he pursued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercantilism">mercantilism</a>, enlarged the state's share of the economy, extorted payments from wealthy Asante to build the reserves, and created the Manwere fekuo, the last of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>'s administrative units. He rebuilt Kumasi's streets and the great roads. In 1837 he agreed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-the-netherlands">William I of the Netherlands</a> to supply recruits for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> Army in exchange for guns, and sent his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwasi-boachi">Kwasi Boachi</a> to be educated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some Asante studied in Europe: in 1837 Kwasi Boachi, a son of <b>Kwaku Dua I</b>, and his cousin Kwame Poku were sent to the Netherlands for a European education.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ashanti">Social Hierarchy of the Ashanti Empire</a></li></ul></section>
